On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:17:14 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> Make sure this is documented in the man page for lab-process-maild.

Done in "[PATCH v3 0/2] lab-process-mail: Add lab-process-maild" [1].
Patch 1 adds lab-process-maild(8) and the package installs it under
share/man/man8, so "man lab-process-maild" finds it once ~/local/bin
is on PATH. It documents start, stop, status, and run, the conf, and
every setting with its default, and has an example conf.

> Yeah here's where I was going with that... a package installs to
> LOCALBASE, but often creates files in /var/run or whatever.
>
> So I goofed, this shouldn't be installing directly to ~. It should have
> a LOCALBASE, which in this case is ~/local.
>
> The package won't create the log of pid files I assume, or even their
> dirs. So I think needs vars for both of those, just like an ordinary
> rc.conf.

Right; the package installs only bin/, share/, and the man page. The
daemon's LOCALBASE is the parent of its bin/, ~/local after "just
install", and only the conf is found there:

    ~/local/etc/lab-process-maild.conf

The log and pidfile each have a variable:

    lab_process_maild_logfile
    lab_process_maild_pidfile

They default to ~/log/lab-process-maild.log and
~/var/run/lab-process-maild.pid, or to /var/log and /var/run when the
daemon runs as root, so neither case needs a conf. start creates their
directories if they are missing, since nothing else will.

> In fact rather than setting env directly, let's configure this like an
> rc script.

It now reads rc.conf(5)-style variables, set but not exported:

    lab_process_maild_env="LAB_BASE_BRANCH=b/dev@upstream \
        NOTIFY_URL=https://ntfy.sh/my-topic"
    lab_process_maild_interval=60

lab_process_maild_env works like ${name}_env in rc.subr(8): the words
are added to the otherwise empty environment of each lab-process-mail
run. As in rc.subr, a value cannot contain a space. run applies it,
not start, so it holds under s6 or any other supervisor too. Any other
variable in the conf stays in the daemon and never reaches
lab-process-mail.

I did not write an actual rc(8) script. rc.subr wants the script in
an rc.d directory that rc(8) reads, which a ~/local install does not
have, and start, stop, and status are already in lab-process-maild.

